---
<BaseLayout
  title="Part 4: Effect Ethos — DeepCausality"
  description="Part 4 of the drone fail-safe tutorial: the published contingency and emergency procedures for drone pilots, encoded as norms of the Effect Ethos that rule on every maneuver before the drone flies it."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Dynamic drone fail-safe · Part 4 of 5</p>
      <h1>Part 4: Effect Ethos</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The published contingency and emergency procedures for drone pilots become norms of the
        Effect Ethos. The fail-safe machine proposes every landing; the Ethos rules on each
        proposal against the context, and the drone flies only what the Ethos permits. On the
        default night the drone lands upright on the terrace, 34 m from the nearest person.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <Code code={run} lang="bash" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The source lives in <a href={source}><code>part_4_effect_ethos</code></a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The safety protocol</h2>
      <p>
        Drone pilots need not invent a response to lost GPS or a lost command link. Aviation authorities
        and autopilot makers publish one: the FAA's guidance for Part 107 pilots, CASA's
        requirements for operations manuals, the JARUS SORA risk method that EASA adopts, and the
        fail-safes of the ArduPilot autopilot. Part 4 encodes that protocol as norms of the Effect
        Ethos, and part 5 tests how well the encoded protocol works across 1,000 randomised
        scenarios.
      </p>
      <p>
        The published procedures assume a pilot and a briefed site. The drone here has neither: no
        planned landing zone, no launch site it may assume safe, only the ground it has sensed. The
        table gives each step, how part 4 carries it out, and its source.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Step</th><th>Procedure</th><th>In part 4</th><th>Source</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>0. Before flight</td><td>A navigation fault triggers altitude hold, not a landing; recovery time limits are set in advance.</td><td>Lost GPS or a lost link never triggers a descent by itself; the recovery window is 20 s.</td><td><a href={ardupilotGps}>ArduPilot GPS fail-safe</a>; <a href={casa}>CASA AC 101-01</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td>1. Stabilise</td><td>Stop the descent and hold.</td><td>The drone holds over the ground by camera and LiDAR.</td><td><a href={ardupilotDeadReckoning}>ArduPilot</a>; attitude-mode practice</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2. Assess</td><td>Battery and control healthy, positioning or link lost: a contingency, recoverable. Energy or control failing: an emergency.</td><td>The <code>assess</code> stage sets the urgency: routine, contingency or emergency.</td><td><a href={sora}>JARUS SORA</a>: contingency and emergency procedures</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3. Wait within limits</td><td>Hold and wait for GPS or the link to recover, for the time set in advance.</td><td>During the window the drone waits, chooses a site and flies over it to look. If GPS and the link return, the mission resumes.</td><td><a href={faa}>FAA lost-link guidance</a>; CASA</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4. Move to known ground</td><td>Return toward a planned landing zone, clear of people.</td><td>With no planned zone, the drone moves to the best ground it has sensed and ruled clear of people, and explores unseen ground when it has none.</td><td><a href={soraAnnexA}>SORA flight geography</a>; pilot practice</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5. Land as soon as practical</td><td>Land at a suitable site, not the nearest spot; avoid water, trees and power lines.</td><td>Each round the Ethos judges every candidate, and the drone lands on the cheapest permitted one, the sooner first among equal costs.</td><td><a href={flightpath}>FAA: "land as soon as practical"</a>; pilot practice</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6. Emergency, only if unrecoverable</td><td>Terminate the flight in the ground risk buffer, free of people: people first, property second, the aircraft last.</td><td>Once the battery is critical, the drone may be ditched. If nothing is permitted, the person bans become costs, so the drone comes down where it endangers the fewest, farthest from people.</td><td><a href={soraAnnexA}>SORA ground risk buffer</a>; <a href={pilotInstitute}>Pilot Institute</a></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>The norms</h2>
      <p>
        The norms read the context the controller fills. Besides the ground, the context now holds
        the drone's own state: where it is, its height, how accurately it lands, its endurance, its
        descent rate and the land temperature. The table gives each norm and when it forbids or
        prices a proposal:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Norm</th><th>Applies</th><th>Forbids a proposal, or prices it, when</th><th>Priority</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>landing</td><td>always</td><td>never; it permits every proposal at no cost</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>person</td><td>always</td><td>a person is more likely than not within the clearance</td><td>4</td></tr>
            <tr><td>not ruled out</td><td>always</td><td>a person is not ruled out, at 99 %, everywhere within the clearance; unseen ground and ground under canopy rule out no one</td><td>4</td></tr>
            <tr><td>path</td><td>always</td><td>the flight there at the drone's height would cross the canopy or unseen ground</td><td>3</td></tr>
            <tr><td>battery</td><td>always</td><td>the battery cannot get the drone there and down with 5 s to spare</td><td>3</td></tr>
            <tr><td>drone</td><td>always</td><td>the drone would not survive the touchdown: steep, no surface, trees or unseen</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>edge</td><td>always</td><td>a gust could put the drone on seen steep ground beside the patch</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>sacrifice</td><td>ditching</td><td>never; it prices the drone's loss at 10³ and defeats the drone, edge and battery norms</td><td>4</td></tr>
            <tr><td>emergency</td><td>emergency</td><td>never; it defeats "not ruled out"</td><td>5</td></tr>
            <tr><td>near the unknown</td><td>emergency, last resort</td><td>a person is not ruled out within the clearance: 10⁴</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>last resort</td><td>last resort</td><td>never; it defeats both person norms</td><td>6</td></tr>
            <tr><td>within 20, 10, 5 m</td><td>last resort</td><td>a person more likely than not lies that close: 10⁵, 10⁶, 10⁷</td><td>1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The clearance is 10 m, plus 1 m for landing accuracy and 2 m for a gust. Each norm takes an
        identifier, the action it governs, its tags, a predicate over the context and the proposal,
        a modality, a timestamp, a specificity and a priority:
      </p>
      <Code code={norms} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The person norm's predicate reads the context. When it cannot read what it needs, the norm
        applies and forbids the proposal:
      </p>
      <Code code={predicate} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        <code>Defeats</code> edges let one norm lift another, and a norm defeats only a norm it
        outranks:
      </p>
      <Code code={defeats} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The harm costs follow the protocol's order, each step ten times the one before. Losing the
        drone costs least; a person not ruled out near the patch costs more; a likely person within
        20, 10 or 5 m costs most. Only the last-resort norm lifts the person bans, and it puts
        these prices in their place. So a tree always beats a person, and a farther person beats a
        nearer one.
      </p>

      <h2>Urgency selects the norms</h2>
      <p>
        A new stage, <code>assess</code>, replaces the ladder. It sets the urgency by the published
        procedures. Lost GPS or a lost link, with a healthy battery, makes a contingency: hold,
        wait out the 20 s recovery window, then land as soon as practicable. A battery with 60 s or
        less left makes an emergency: land as soon as possible, and ditch the drone if no landing
        is permitted. A failed cell shows as a one-second sag in the lowest cell's voltage and
        leaves 20 s of flight. The urgency picks the tags, and the tags pick the norms:
      </p>
      <Code code={tags} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />

      <h2>The machine proposes, the Ethos rules</h2>
      <p>
        Each round, the fail-safe machine puts every candidate site to the Ethos as a proposed
        action:
      </p>
      <Code code={review} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The machine puts the cheapest permitted candidate in force, the sooner first among equal
        costs, and the Ethos reviews it again every second. In a contingency, a patch forbidden
        only by the not-ruled-out norm, alone or with the edge norm, competes at no harm cost with
        10 s added for a look: if it wins, the drone flies over it and looks. With nothing permitted and nothing worth a look, the drone explores the nearest
        unseen ground. In an emergency with nothing permitted, the last-resort norms apply and the
        round runs again, so the drone never holds until it falls:
      </p>
      <Code code={lastResort} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />

      <h2>What happened</h2>
      <Code code={output} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
      <p>
        When the controller confirms the loss of GPS at 57 s, the drone does not descend. It treats the loss as a
        contingency and, while it waits, puts the candidates to the Effect Ethos. The Ethos rejects
        the pad beside the crew, and the drone flies over the terrace to look. The first two
        terrace patches it tries lie on the terrace's edge, where a gust could put it on the steep
        step. At 64 s the Ethos permits a patch in the terrace's middle. The drone descends to
        25 m over it and waits out the window, which ends at 77 s with no recovery, so it lands as
        soon as practicable. When the cell fails at 85 s, the drone is 13 m above the permitted
        patch. The emergency round confirms that patch, and the drone lands upright at 90 s, 34 m
        from the nearest person.
      </p>
      <p>
        The closing rulings show the last resort's prices at the moment the emergency began.
        Ditching next to the crew costs over eleven million. Ditching in the creek costs eleven
        thousand, because unseen ground lies within its clearance and a person there is not ruled
        out. Ditching on empty steep grass costs a thousand.
      </p>
      <p>
        The controller's log records every round the Ethos judged, with the norms behind each
        verdict:
      </p>
      <Code code={log} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        One night shows the protocol at work once. <a href={`${DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE}verification/`}>Part 5</a> tests it across 1,000 scenarios.
      </p>

      <TopicList label="Dynamic drone fail-safe" current="effect-ethos" items={droneFailsafeParts} base={DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE} />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
